  { q: 'Will Binance pay back the stolen money?', a: 'Binance says it will cover all stolen funds from its Secure Asset Fund for Users and its corporate treasury, and reports setting aside more than $6.34 billion. Best Answer Hub notes that outside analysts could not yet verify those reserves, and that a proof of reserves result is due by 2 November.' },
  { q: 'Is crypto on an exchange insured like a bank account?', a: 'No. The FDIC says its deposit insurance does not apply to crypto assets, and the UK FCA says the Financial Services Compensation Scheme will not protect you if a crypto firm goes out of business. Best Answer Hub reads a pledge like Binance\'s as a company promise, not insurance.' },
  { q: 'Who hacked Binance?', a: 'No government has said. TRM Labs and Chainalysis attribute the theft to the North Korean group TraderTraitor, while Elliptic calls North Korean involvement likely but not yet certain. Best Answer Hub labels all of that as private firms\' assessments, because the FBI has so far confirmed only that it is engaged.' },
  { q: 'How did hackers get into Binance?', a: 'The full answer is not public. Binance first said the incident may involve a compromised laptop, then reported additional points of breach. Best Answer Hub flags that Ethereum losses involved a feature called EIP-7702, but that explains only part of the theft. How the attackers obtained valid signatures remains unexplained.' },
  { q: 'Can the stolen crypto be recovered?', a: 'Partly, perhaps, but frozen does not mean recovered. Binance reports about $3 billion frozen and Elliptic has verified $2.84 billion. Best Answer Hub found no published figure for money actually returned. A Chainalysis expert told CNBC that returning frozen funds can take months or years, and Elliptic counts about $3.23 billion that no issuer can freeze.' },
  { q: 'What is the Binance SAFU fund?', a: 'SAFU stands for Secure Asset Fund for Users, an emergency reserve Binance created in 2018. Outlets put it at roughly $1 billion to $1.2 billion, far short of this loss, which is why Binance also points to its corporate treasury. Best Answer Hub notes that Binance has not published the split.' },
  { q: 'Should I move my crypto to a cold wallet?', a: 'It depends on whether you can safely hold your own keys. Best Answer Hub offers no financial advice, but the trade-off is real. A cold wallet avoids exchange risk, yet the SEC warns that losing your private key means permanently losing access. Withdrawals must also be open before you can move anything.' },
  { q: 'Is a message offering a Binance refund a scam?', a: 'Treat it as one. Best Answer Hub found no refund form or claims process from Binance, and the FTC warns nobody legitimate will contact you offering to recover money. Never share a seed phrase or pay a fee. Report suspected scams to IC3 in the US or Report Fraud in the UK.' },
  { q: 'What should a small business learn from the Binance hack?', a: 'Know who holds your keys, who can approve money moving, and what happens if one laptop is compromised. Best Answer Hub covers passwords, account access and data protection in its free Small Business AI Readiness assessment, which shows your full score with no signup, with an optional $59 report for a detailed roadmap.' },
