      <article>
        <p class="lead">On 29 October 2026, attackers drained hot wallets at Binance, the exchange at the center of the crypto market, taking about $6.3 billion by Binance's estimate. <strong>It is the largest crypto theft on record by value at the time it was taken. Binance promises customers will not bear the loss, and that promise has not yet been tested: when this guide was last checked, withdrawals were still suspended and the proof of reserves was still pending.</strong></p>

        <p>This Best Answer Hub guide keeps three things apart: what Binance and independent analysts have confirmed, what companies claim, and what nobody knows yet. Numbers are moving, so each one carries its source and time. Nothing here is financial advice.</p>

        <div class="cav">Start here</div>
        <h2 id="what">What happened in the Binance hack?</h2>

        <p>Best Answer Hub's short version: attackers took control of Binance hot wallets, which are the internet-connected wallets an exchange uses to process withdrawals, and emptied them across about 11 blockchains within a few hours. Binance states that "At around 2026-10-29 20:05 (UTC), our hot wallets were compromised, resulting in a loss of approximately $6.3B USD worth of token assets" (<a href="https://www.binance.com/en/support/announcement/detail/d3306d9dca5243548c813b605e1d1751" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Binance Update #2, 30 October 2026</a>). In US Eastern time, that was 4:05 p.m. on Thursday 29 October. In Asia and Australia it was already Friday, which is why some reports date the hack 30 October.</p>

        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Time (UTC)</th><th>US Eastern</th><th>What happened</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="band">29 Oct, about 20:05</td><td>4:05 p.m.</td><td>First outflows: five Binance wallets on the XRP Ledger send 233 million XRP to one address (Elliptic)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">29 Oct, 20:24</td><td>4:24 p.m.</td><td>$2.53 billion leaves one Ethereum hot wallet in a single transaction (Elliptic)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">29 Oct, 21:01 to 21:04</td><td>5:01 to 5:04 p.m.</td><td>Tether and Circle start freezing stolen stablecoins (TRM Labs)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">29 Oct, about 22:30</td><td>About 6:30 p.m.</td><td>Binance publicly says withdrawals will resume once the issue is resolved. The exact moment of the halt is unclear</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">30 Oct, 02:23</td><td>29 Oct, 10:23 p.m.</td><td>Binance publishes its first security notice, citing about $6.3 billion</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">30 Oct, 19:33</td><td>3:33 p.m.</td><td>Binance's second notice reports additional points of breach and more than $6.34 billion set aside</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">1 Nov, about 05:33</td><td>1:33 a.m.</td><td>Withdrawal restart target. Best Answer Hub's arithmetic from "within approximately 34 hours"; phased and subject to checks</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">By 2 Nov</td><td>No time given</td><td>Binance's promised proof of reserves result</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="callout peach"><div class="h">Why the withdrawal countdown looks like it keeps changing</div><p>Reports have said 48, 45, 40 and 34 hours. By Best Answer Hub's arithmetic, those are not delays. They are countdowns issued at different moments, and they land in the same window early on Sunday 1 November UTC. Binance itself calls this a target and says the restart will be phased.</p></div>

        <div class="cav">The number everyone is quoting</div>
        <h2 id="amount">How much was stolen from Binance?</h2>

        <p>Best Answer Hub reports a range of about $6.3 billion to $6.43 billion, because each figure measures something slightly different. Binance has not published its method, and the two analytics firms that have explained theirs both price coins at the moment they were taken. The roughly 1% gap between those two is not fully explained, partly because Elliptic counts 11 blockchains and TRM Labs counts ten.</p>

