        <p>The Binance figure of $6.43 billion compares with $1.46 billion for Bybit in 2025, about $620 million for Ronin in 2022, about $610 million for Poly Network in 2021 and about $530 million for Coincheck in 2018. Three cases can muddy the comparison, and Best Answer Hub would rather name them than let them surprise you.</p>

        <div class="sec">
          <ul class="pillars">
            <li><span class="dot">1</span><div><b>Today's prices change the ranking.</b> The about 647,000 BTC that US prosecutors link to the Mt. Gox theft would be worth about $50 billion at about $77,300 per bitcoin. Bitfinex's 119,754 BTC, about $70 million in 2016, would be about $9.26 billion. That is Best Answer Hub's arithmetic, using a 30 October price.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">2</span><div><b>LuBian is contested.</b> About 127,426 BTC, worth about $3.5 billion, left the LuBian mining operation around the end of 2020. Elliptic says it is unclear "whether a theft really took place" (<a href="https://www.elliptic.co/insights/15-billion-seized-by-us-originates-from-iranchina-bitcoin-miner-theft" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Elliptic, 2025</a>).</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">3</span><div><b>FTX was not a hack.</b> FTX's losses of about $8 billion in customer money were a fraud and insolvency case, not a theft by outsiders. So "biggest theft" is defensible, while "biggest crypto loss ever" is not.</div></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p>Freezes narrow the gap too. Subtracting the $2.84 billion Elliptic has verified as frozen leaves roughly $3.59 billion, by Best Answer Hub's rough arithmetic, which mixes time-of-theft and current prices. Those freezes are not necessarily permanent either.</p>

        <div class="cav">Confirmed, claimed, unknown</div>
        <h2 id="how">How did hackers get into Binance?</h2>

        <p>Best Answer Hub's honest answer is that the full attack path is not public, and early headlines blaming "one laptop" are out of date. Binance's first notice said the incident "may involve" a compromised engineer's laptop. Its second says, "Our initial investigation suggested that the incident may involve a compromised laptop, however, our subsequent investigation has uncovered additional points of breach." It has not named those points.</p>

        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Claim</th><th>Status</th><th>Who says so</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="band">Hot wallets on multiple chains were compromised</td><td><span class="pill lv1">Confirmed</span></td><td>Binance, Elliptic, TRM Labs</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">EIP-7702, an Ethereum account-delegation feature, was used on Ethereum</td><td><span class="pill lv1">Confirmed</span></td><td>Binance's co-CEO, on-chain analysts. It explains only part of the loss</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">A compromised laptop played a role</td><td><span class="pill lv2">Claimed, hedged</span></td><td>Binance ("may involve")</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">No wallet private keys leaked</td><td><span class="pill lv2">Claimed</span></td><td>Binance: "no evidence" at this stage</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Binance's own systems were made to sign for the attacker</td><td><span class="pill lv2">Analysts' inference</span></td><td>Elliptic ("appears"), other analysts. Binance has not said so</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">An insider helped, or a supplier was the way in</td><td><span class="pill lv3">Unknown</span></td><td>No source reviewed alleges it, and none rules it out</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>The important technical point is narrower than it sounds. CoinDesk's analysis describes about $2.5 billion leaving Ethereum through an EIP-7702 delegation, with more swept later, while bitcoin, XRP, Solana and Tron losses left through ordinary signed transfers (<a href="https://www.coindesk.com/tech/2026/10/30/here-s-how-one-ethereum-permission-let-an-attacker-drain-usd2-5-billion-from-binance" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CoinDesk, 2026</a>). So any explanation that says "EIP-7702 caused the $6.3 billion hack" overreaches. Binance has promised further technical findings within about 48 hours of its 30 October notice, followed by a detailed report.</p>

        <p>Government advisories describe why people, not encryption, tend to be the way in. A joint US advisory says North Korean intrusions against crypto firms "begin with a large number of spearphishing messages sent to employees", often mimicking high-paying job offers (<a href="https://www.cisa.gov/news-events/cybersecurity-advisories/aa22-108a" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CISA, FBI and Treasury, 2022</a>). That is background, not a finding about Binance, since the entry point here remains undisclosed.</p>

        <div class="cta">
          <span class="kicker">Building something with AI? Same lesson, smaller scale</span>
