        <div class="cav">Assessment is not proof</div>
        <h2 id="who">Did North Korea hack Binance?</h2>

        <p>Three private analytics firms point to North Korea, but no government has confirmed it, as of the latest statements Best Answer Hub could find. The firms do not all express the same confidence, so the exact wording matters. The term TraderTraitor comes from US government reporting on a North Korean state-sponsored hacking cluster (<a href="https://www.ic3.gov/PSA/2025/PSA250226" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FBI, 2025</a>).</p>

        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>What they say</th><th>Type</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="band">TRM Labs</td><td>"TRM attributes the attack to TraderTraitor" with "high confidence", partly on off-chain intelligence it has not disclosed</td><td>Vendor</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Chainalysis</td><td>"Chainalysis attributes the hack to TraderTraitor, a North Korean cybercrime group."</td><td>Vendor</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Elliptic</td><td>DPRK-linked actors "likely", "although the attribution is not yet certain"</td><td>Vendor</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">FBI</td><td>"aware of the incident" and engaged with partners. No attribution</td><td>Government</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Binance</td><td>"Our investigation has not attributed responsibility to any individual."</td><td>Victim</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>The strongest public evidence is a link to the September theft from Bitget, an exchange that lost about $388 million. Funds from both thefts were combined, as investigator ZachXBT first reported and as Elliptic and Chainalysis confirmed, though Elliptic says "Only a small proportion" of the Binance funds is involved so far. What that proves is disputed. Chainalysis treats it as strengthening its assessment. Elliptic reads the same evidence more cautiously: "Laundering networks can serve more than one client, so the link does not prove that the same hacker carried out both thefts."</p>

        <div class="callout"><div class="h">Two date traps to avoid</div><p>The FBI statement naming North Korea and TraderTraitor that many stories mention is about Bybit in 2025, not Binance. It took the FBI five days to name North Korea in that case, so silence at this stage says little either way. And the US Treasury sanctions on North Korean IT workers announced on 28 October came the day before this hack, so they are not a response to it.</p></div>

        <div class="cav">The freeze is not the finish</div>
        <h2 id="recover">Can the stolen Binance crypto be recovered?</h2>

        <p>Some of it may be, but Best Answer Hub found no published figure for money actually returned. Stablecoin issuers can freeze their own tokens, and they moved quickly: Tether froze 971 million USDT at 21:01 UTC, about an hour after the first outflows, with Circle following at 21:04 (<a href="https://www.trmlabs.com/resources/blog/north-korea-steals-usd-6-36-billion-from-binance-in-the-largest-hack-in-history" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TRM Labs, 2026</a>). Binance reports about $3 billion frozen. Elliptic has verified $2.84 billion, as of about 12:55 UTC on 30 October.</p>

        <blockquote>"A freeze stops the money from moving, and recovery follows through <span class="accent">legal process.</span>" <small style="display:block;font-family:Karla,sans-serif;font-size:.8rem;font-weight:600;color:#847B6B;margin-top:10px">Ari Redbord, TRM Labs, to CNBC, 30 October 2026</small></blockquote>

        <p>Bitcoin and ether are different, because no issuer can freeze them. Elliptic counts about $3.23 billion, at current prices, that the attacker still controlled and no issuer can freeze, including 18,270 BTC split across 19 addresses, none yet sent to a service as of its last update. Elliptic says those funds "can now only be stopped when they reach an exchange or other service". Eric Jardine of Chainalysis told CNBC, "There is no standard recovery timeline. A technical freeze may happen quickly, while returning funds can take months or years" (<a href="https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/30/billions-in-crypto-frozen-in-binance-hack-heres-what-happens-next-.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CNBC, 2026</a>). Elliptic expects laundering to take months, as it did after Bybit. That is a forecast, not a fact.</p>

        <div class="cav">The question you came for</div>
        <h2 id="safe">Is my money safe on Binance?</h2>

        <p>Best Answer Hub's honest reading: Binance has made a strong promise and has not yet been able to prove it. The company says, "Binance will absorb the financial impact of these stolen funds, not our users." It reports setting aside more than $6.34 billion in company funds, including $5 billion "held in the exact impacted token assets", which it describes as covering approximately 80% of the impact, while it buys the remainder.</p>

        <p>Here is where that promise currently stands, row by row.</p>

        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Your question</th><th>Where it stands</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="band">Can I withdraw?</td><td>Suspended when last checked. Binance targets starting a phased restart by about 05:33 UTC on 1 November (Best Answer Hub's arithmetic), subject to security checks</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Can I deposit?</td><td>Binance says deposits are open. It first reported them suspended, then edited its notice to say they remained unaffected</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Was my account accessed?</td><td>Not established. NBC reported it was not immediately clear whether individual accounts or holdings were accessed</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Whose money was stolen?</td><td>Contested. Co-CEO Richard Teng says, "We never use any customer funds, so they are not impacted." CoinDesk found those wallets had been listed as backing customer balances</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Are the reserves real?</td><td>Unverified. A Binance spokesperson declined to share wallet addresses for the $5 billion, citing security (<a href="https://cointelegraph.com/news/binance-5-billion-replacement-crypto-in-custody" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cointelegraph</a>); a proof of reserves result is due by 2 November</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Does this affect Binance.US?</td><td>Binance.US says it is separate and not affected, per ABC News</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>The company's scale gives the promise some weight: CoinDesk, citing DefiLlama, puts about $144 billion on the platform before the hack, making the loss roughly 4% of that. Fundstrat's Sean Farrell told CNBC that the "key tell will be how quickly withdrawals resume and whether outflows stay orderly." Best Answer Hub agrees that this is the real test, because a customer run cannot show up while withdrawals are switched off.</p>

        <div class="sec">
          <ul class="pillars">
            <li><span class="dot">1</span><div><b>Check status in the app, not through links.</b> Binance says, "For verified updates, please follow Binance's official channels only, or contact us directly through the official Customer Support." Type the address or open the app yourself.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">2</span><div><b>Do not panic-sell because of a headline.</b> Binance says users will not bear the loss. Whether to hold, sell or move is your decision, and it should not be made because a stranger messaged you.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">3</span><div><b>Secure your own login.</b> Use a unique password and app-based two-factor authentication. The free Best Answer Hub <a href="/tools/password-generator/">Password Generator</a> runs in your browser, so the password is not sent anywhere.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">4</span><div><b>Save your records now.</b> Keep your balance screenshots and transaction history. The IRS expects US taxpayers to keep digital asset records, and they would matter in any later dispute. Ask a tax professional about anything beyond that.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">5</span><div><b>Watch two dates.</b> The withdrawal restart on 1 November and the proof of reserves by 2 November are the first real evidence. Keep in mind that a proof of reserves is a snapshot, and the US audit regulator PCAOB warns such reports are not audits.</div></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="cav">The promise versus the guarantee</div>
        <h2 id="insured">Is crypto on an exchange insured like a bank account?</h2>

        <p>No, and Best Answer Hub considers this the most important fact in the story for everyday users. If a promise like Binance's fails, the government safety nets you may assume exist do not apply. SAFU, Binance's emergency fund, is a company-run reserve rather than insurance, and outlets size it at only about $1 billion to $1.2 billion.</p>

        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Safety net</th><th>Does it cover exchange crypto?</th><th>In the regulator's words</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="band">FDIC (US banks)</td><td><span class="pill lv3">No</span></td><td>"FDIC deposit insurance does not apply to" products including "crypto assets" (<a href="https://www.fdic.gov/news/fact-sheets/crypto-fact-sheet-7-28-22.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FDIC</a>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">SIPC (US brokers)</td><td><span class="pill lv3">Mostly no</span></td><td>FINRA says crypto assets that are not securities under the law are not protected (<a href="https://www.finra.org/investors/investing/investment-products/crypto-assets/risks" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FINRA</a>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">FSCS (UK)</td><td><span class="pill lv3">No</span></td><td>"You also won't be protected by the Financial Services Compensation Scheme (FSCS) if the firm goes out of business." (<a href="https://www.fca.org.uk/consumers/crypto-investment-scams" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FCA, 2026</a>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">US government</td><td><span class="pill lv3">No duty</span></td><td>"the government has no obligation to step in and help get your money back." (<a href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/what-know-about-cryptocurrency-and-scams" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FTC</a>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">SAFU (Binance)</td><td><span class="pill lv2">Company fund</span></td><td>Binance's own reserve, with no government backing that Best Answer Hub could find</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>"Made whole" has also meant very different things in past hacks. Binance covered its $40 million loss in 2019 from SAFU. Bybit said it honored withdrawals after 2025. Poly Network's attacker returned the money in 2021. Bitfinex imposed an approximately 36% haircut across accounts in 2016 and repaid through tokens. Mt. Gox has largely completed its main repayments more than a decade on, with the deadline for the remainder now 31 October 2027.</p>

        <div class="cav">Do not swap one risk for another blindly</div>
        <h2 id="wallet">Should I move my crypto to a cold wallet?</h2>

        <p>Best Answer Hub won't tell you what to do with your money, but it will show you the trade honestly, because "not your keys, not your coins" only tells half of it. Holding your own keys removes exchange risk and hands you every other risk. Note that a cold wallet is a device kept offline, whereas a hot wallet stays connected to the internet.</p>

        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Risk</th><th>Keep on an exchange</th><th>Hold your own keys</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="band">Exchange hack or failure</td><td>Depends on the firm's promise and finances</td><td>Not directly exposed</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Withdrawal freezes</td><td>Can happen, as at Binance now</td><td>Not applicable</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Lost key or seed phrase</td><td>Usually a support process</td><td>"you permanently lose access" (SEC)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Device lost or damaged</td><td>Not applicable</td><td>May cause permanent loss without a backup</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Scams</td><td>Fake support, account takeover</td><td>Seed phrase phishing, signing malicious transactions</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Two facts stop this from being a simple choice. Individuals are not safe merely by leaving exchanges: Chainalysis counted at least $713 million stolen from personal wallets across about 158,000 incidents in 2025 (<a href="https://www.chainalysis.com/blog/crypto-hacking-stolen-funds-2026/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Chainalysis, 2025</a>). And "cold" is not a magic word: the FBI-attributed Bybit theft reportedly hit a multisignature cold wallet, after signers were shown a falsified interface. The SEC's custody bulletin is a sensible next read, and it covers the sole responsibility you take on with your own keys (<a href="https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins/crypto-asset-custody-basics-retail-investors-investor-bulletin-0" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SEC staff, 2025</a>).</p>

        <div class="callout sage"><div class="h">The coaching point</div><p>Do not rush this, even once withdrawals reopen. A hurried first-time transfer is exactly where people send coins to the wrong address or fall for a fake wallet app. If you decide to move, Best Answer Hub suggests buying hardware only from the manufacturer, writing the seed phrase on paper, and sending a small test amount first.</p></div>

        <div class="cav">Expect this next</div>
        <h2 id="scams">What Binance scams should I watch for after the hack?</h2>

        <p>Best Answer Hub wants to be precise here: security firms are warning that scams are likely, but no documented Binance refund campaign had been verified when this guide was last checked. The warnings still deserve attention. Binance said users "should not trust unofficial links, messages, or requests for account information", and Bitdefender says "users should expect fake support messages, compensation offers, and withdrawal links exploiting the headlines" (<a href="https://www.bitdefender.com/en-us/blog/hotforsecurity/binance-hack-6-3-billion-laptop-breach" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Bitdefender, 2026</a>).</p>

        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>The pitch</th><th>Why it should stop you</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="band">"Claim your Binance refund here"</td><td>Binance describes absorbing the loss itself. Best Answer Hub found no claims form or process</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">"Verify your wallet or seed phrase"</td><td>Nobody genuine needs your seed phrase. Sharing it can hand over everything in that wallet</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">"Withdrawals have reopened, click to move funds"</td><td>Open the app yourself and look. Do not use the link</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">"Pay a small fee to recover your funds"</td><td>"Don't pay anyone who contacts you, offering to recover money" (<a href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/consumer-alerts/2022/11/worried-about-crypto-exchange-losses-dont-pay-money-help-recovering-money" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FTC</a>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">"Law firm working with the FBI"</td><td>"There are no law firms which are officially authorized partners of US Government agencies." (<a href="https://www.ic3.gov/PSA/2025/PSA250813" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FBI</a>)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>These are not small risks. The FBI's Internet Crime Complaint Center logged 181,565 cryptocurrency-related complaints and about $11.37 billion in losses in 2025 (<a href="https://www.ic3.gov/AnnualReport/Reports/2025_IC3Report.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IC3, 2025</a>).</p>

        <div class="sec">
          <ul class="pillars">
            <li><span class="dot">1</span><div><b>If you sent money, act fast.</b> The FTC says to contact the exchange immediately, tell them it was a fraudulent transaction, and ask them to reverse it. Crypto payments lack card-style protections, so recovery can be hard (<a href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/what-do-if-you-were-scammed" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FTC, 2026</a>).</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">2</span><div><b>US: report it.</b> Use <a href="https://www.ic3.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ic3.gov</a> and <a href="https://reportfraud.ftc.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ReportFraud.ftc.gov</a>.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">3</span><div><b>UK: report it.</b> In England, Wales and Northern Ireland, use <a href="https://www.reportfraud.police.uk/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Report Fraud</a>, which replaced Action Fraud in December 2025. In Scotland, call Police Scotland on 101. Elsewhere, contact local police or your financial regulator.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">4</span><div><b>Keep everything.</b> Save messages, addresses and transaction IDs. Beware of anyone who then offers to get the money back for a fee, because that is often a second scam.</div></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

