        <div class="cav">If you run a business</div>
        <h2 id="business">What should a small business learn from the Binance hack?</h2>

        <p>Best Answer Hub takes three lessons from this story that apply even if your business has never touched crypto. Each comes down to who can move what, and what happens when one person or machine gets compromised.</p>

        <div class="sec">
          <ul class="pillars">
            <li><span class="dot">1</span><div><b>Know who holds your keys.</b> List the accounts that can move money, change payment details or export customer data, and who controls them. If the answer is "one person's laptop", fix that first.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">2</span><div><b>Ask what your approvals actually check.</b> Teng said the malicious delegation "did not pass through our normal approval controls". Whatever the final report shows, an approval process only protects you if it cannot be routed around.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">3</span><div><b>Size your dependence on any one provider.</b> Binance customers lost the ability to withdraw within hours, even with a repayment promise. Ask what your business would do if a bank, payment processor or exchange froze access for a week.</div></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

