        <div class="cav">The wider ripple</div>
        <h2 id="fallout">How did markets and regulators react to the Binance hack?</h2>

        <p>Best Answer Hub's summary: prices dipped sharply, regulators mostly spoke in general terms, and no enforcement action or lawsuit had surfaced when this guide was checked. Bitcoin fell to an overnight low of about $76,400 and ether to about $2,170, while BNB dropped 6.2% over 24 hours and liquidations reached about $1.57 billion (<a href="https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/10/30/crypto-liquidations-jump-to-usd1-57-billion-as-binance-hack-sends-bitcoin-ether-lower" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CoinDesk, 30 October 2026</a>). NBC notes the drop extended a month-long decline, so not all of it can be pinned on the hack.</p>

        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>What they said or did</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="band">Abu Dhabi FSRA</td><td>Binance's main licensing regulator said it is "engaging closely with Binance and monitoring the incident"</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">CFTC Chair Michael Selig</td><td>"The real issue here is that these are unregulated offshore exchanges, and they don't have the same sorts of safeguards" (via Decrypt)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">FBI, US Secret Service</td><td>Both confirmed involvement in the response. Neither named a culprit</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Sen. Cynthia Lummis</td><td>Argued the Clarity Act would have helped by giving firms legal cover to freeze suspicious transactions</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Better Markets</td><td>Disputes that. Amanda Fischer says the bill would have required issuers to "freeze per a court order". Patrick Wilson of the Solana Policy Institute replies, "No court order required" (via The Block)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>One caution on Binance's past. It agreed to a $4.3 billion plea deal in 2023 over money-laundering and sanctions controls. That is real history, but Best Answer Hub found no credible source tying that case to the cause of this hack, so it should not be read as an explanation.</p>

        <div class="cav">Keep this list handy</div>
        <h2 id="unknown">What is still unknown about the Binance hack?</h2>

        <p>Quite a lot, and Best Answer Hub would rather list the gaps than fill them with guesses. Treat any confident claim on these points with care until a primary source backs it up.</p>

        <div class="sec">
          <ul class="pillars">
            <li><span class="dot">?</span><div><b>The root cause.</b> What the additional breach points are, and how the attackers obtained valid signatures.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">?</span><div><b>Who did it, officially.</b> No government attribution had been made when this guide was last checked.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">?</span><div><b>Whether the reserves are as described.</b> The proof of reserves result is due by 2 November.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">?</span><div><b>When customers can fully withdraw.</b> The restart is phased, and the order of tokens is unknown.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">?</span><div><b>How much comes back.</b> No recovered amount has been published, and it is unclear how returned funds would be treated.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">?</span><div><b>Whether any customer has lost money.</b> Binance says not; independent confirmation awaits.</div></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

